<script lang="ts">
	import { examples } from '../../utils/examples'
</script>
<template>
	<DocsPage
		product="Meeko i18n"
		locale="en"
		title="Plurals and selectors"
		description="Choose a form based on a quantity or an alternative of the message."
	>
		<h2 id="plural">Plurals with Intl.PluralRules</h2>
		<DocsCode
			label="JSON catalog"
			bind:code="examples.plural"
		/>
		<p>
			<code>plural</code>
			names the numeric argument. An exact match such as
			<code>=0</code>
			is looked up first, then the category of
			<code>Intl.PluralRules</code>
			and finally
			<code>other</code>. The number must be finite.
		</p>
		<p>
			The runtime passes
			<code>count</code>
			to the forms as an alias of that number. When the selector is called
			<code>total</code>, passing
			<code>{'{ total: 3 }'}</code>
			is enough. If the selector is called
			<code>count</code>, that same argument is still required.
		</p>
		<h2 id="select">Alternatives with select</h2>
		<DocsCode
			label="Catalog and call"
			bind:code="examples.select"
		/>
		<p>
			The selector is converted to text to look up its form; a value without its own alternative uses
			<code>other</code>. The parameters used by any of its forms are part of the required contract.
		</p>
		<h2 id="categories">Categories of each language</h2>
		<p>
			Languages may need different categories. Arabic uses zero, one, two, few, many and other; the playground catalog includes those forms. Every plural or select structure requires
			<code>other</code>. The forms contain text.
		</p>
		<UILink to="/playground">Try quantities and permissions</UILink>
	</DocsPage>
</template>
